API inspection checklist

Run this every time, straight after fetch_json, before writing any function.

1. Did it load?
 Is it None? If so, the URL, the internet or the status code is wrong. Stop here.
2. What shape is it?
 type(data): a list or a dict? This decides whether you loop over it or use a key.
 len(data): how many items? Is that what you expected?
 If it’s a dict: check data.keys(). The list you want is often inside one key, e.g. data['results'].
3. What does ONE item look like?
 print(data[0]): look at a single item
 data[0].keys(): copy the key names exactly (userId, not user_id; lng, not long)
 Nesting: which values are themselves a { dict or a [ list? Draw the levels as a comment.
4. Are the types what you need?
 Numbers: a real number, or text that looks like one ('-37.31')? Check with type(...). Text needs safe_float/safe_int.
 True/False: a real True, or the text 'true'?
 Missing or empty values: do some items have '', None, or a missing key?
5. Is it the same in every item?
 Print 2–3 items (data[:3]), not just the first. Do they all have the same keys?
6. With 2 APIs, what links them?
 Which key in API A matches which key in API B? (e.g. todo userId → user id)
 Are both the same type? A number 5 doesn’t match the text '5'.
7. Write it down
 A shape comment at the top of your notes file, e.g.
# todos = list of 200 dicts: {userId: int, id: int, title: str, completed: bool}

The code to paste into your notes file each time:

python
print(data is None)
print(type(data), len(data))
print(data[0])
print(data[0].keys())
print(data[:3])
print(type(data[0]['SOME_KEY']))     # check any value you'll do maths with